# Managed Sensors

This tutorial uses `Jido.Plugin.SensorManager` to run input processes that an Agent starts and stops from its own Actions. A sensor turns outside events into Signals. The Plugin keeps the set of running sensors equal to the set in Agent state.

Jido has no sensor behaviour to implement. A sensor is any OTP child that accepts a `Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Init` value and casts Signals to its Agent. For a fixed set of input processes that the Agent never changes, see [Supervised Resources](../plugins/plugin-supervised-resources.livemd).

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "mikehostetler/new_jido", branch: "main"}
])
```

## Start a Jido instance

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start_link(name: SensorGuide.Jido)

defmodule SensorGuide.Wait do
  def until(check, attempts \\ 200) do
    cond do
      value = check.() ->
        value

      attempts == 0 ->
        raise "condition was not met in time"

      true ->
        Process.sleep(10)
        until(check, attempts - 1)
    end
  end
end
```

## Write a sensor

This sensor receives pushed mailbox events and casts each one to the Agent as a Signal. It stands in for a process that would read from a webhook, a queue, or a socket. `start_link/1` receives the `Init` value. The global name lets this tutorial find the current process after a restart.

The stub fails to start while the `:mailbox_online` setting is `false`, which a later step uses.

```elixir
defmodule SensorGuide.MailboxSensor do
  use GenServer

  alias Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Init

  def start_link(%Init{} = init) do
    if Application.get_env(:sensor_guide, :mailbox_online, true) do
      GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, init, name: {:global, name(init.agent_id, init.tag)})
    else
      {:error, :mailbox_offline}
    end
  end

  def whereis(agent_id, tag) do
    case :global.whereis_name(name(agent_id, tag)) do
      :undefined -> nil
      pid -> pid
    end
  end

  def push(sensor, event), do: GenServer.cast(sensor, {:push, event})

  @impl true
  def init(%Init{} = init), do: {:ok, init}

  @impl true
  def handle_cast({:push, event}, %Init{} = init) do
    signal = Jido.Signal.new!("mail.received", event, source: init.config.source)
    Jido.AgentServer.cast(init.agent_server, signal)
    {:noreply, init}
  end

  defp name(agent_id, tag), do: {__MODULE__, agent_id, tag}
end
```

`GenServer` already provides the `child_spec/1` that the Plugin requires. The Plugin overrides the child ID and sets `restart: :temporary`, because it does the restarting itself.

The `Init` value has these fields:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `agent_server` | PID of the owning AgentServer. Cast Signals here. |
| `agent_id` | ID of the owning Agent. |
| `tag` | The tag from the Directive. |
| `module` | The sensor module. |
| `config` | The config map from the Directive. |
| `jido` | The Jido instance, when there is one. |
| `partition` | The Agent's partition, when there is one. |

## Add the Plugin and manage sensors from an Action

`Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.start/3` and `Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.stop/1` build Directives. Return them from an Action like any other Directive. `:retry_delay_ms` is the wait before retrying a failed start or restarting a crashed sensor. It defaults to `1_000`, and it must be an integer from 1 through 4,294,967,295.

```elixir
defmodule SensorGuide.ManageMailbox do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "sensor_guide_manage_mailbox",
    schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        operation: Jido.Joi.enum([:start, :stop]),
        mailbox: Jido.Joi.string()
      })

  alias Jido.Plugin.SensorManager

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{operation: :start, mailbox: mailbox}, context) do
    directive =
      SensorManager.start(mailbox, SensorGuide.MailboxSensor, %{source: "/mail/#{mailbox}"})

    {:ok, context.agent_state, [directive]}
  end

  def run(%{operation: :stop, mailbox: mailbox}, context) do
    {:ok, context.agent_state, [SensorManager.stop(mailbox)]}
  end
end

defmodule SensorGuide.Inbox do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "sensor_guide_inbox",
    plugins: [{Jido.Plugin.SensorManager, %{retry_delay_ms: 20}}]

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{message_ids: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.string()) |> Jido.Joi.default([])})
  end

  routes do
    route "mailbox.manage", SensorGuide.ManageMailbox

    route "mail.received" do
      action %{message_id: message_id},
        schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{message_id: Jido.Joi.string()}),
        context: context do
        state = context.agent_state

        if message_id in state.message_ids do
          {:ok, state}
        else
          {:ok, %{state | message_ids: state.message_ids ++ [message_id]}}
        end
      end
    end
  end
end

{:ok, inbox} = Jido.start_agent(SensorGuide.Jido, SensorGuide.Inbox, id: "inbox-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(inbox)

manage = fn operation, mailbox ->
  Jido.Signal.new!("mailbox.manage", %{operation: operation, mailbox: mailbox}, source: "/mail/admin")
end
```

## Start a sensor

The Turn commits the desired sensor to the `:sensors` key of Agent state. After the commit, the Plugin starts the process.

```elixir
{:ok, committed, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(inbox, manage.(:start, "support"))

committed.state.sensors.desired
# => %{"support" => %{config: %{source: "/mail/support"}, module: SensorGuide.MailboxSensor}}
```

Push events into the sensor. They arrive through the normal Signal path, so each one is a Turn. A repeated event ID changes nothing, because the handler checks it.

```elixir
sensor = SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn -> SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.whereis("inbox-1", "support") end)

for message_id <- ["msg-1", "msg-2", "msg-2"] do
  SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.push(sensor, %{message_id: message_id})
end

SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn -> length(Jido.AgentServer.agent(inbox).state.message_ids) == 2 end)
Jido.AgentServer.agent(inbox).state.message_ids
# => ["msg-1", "msg-2"]
```

## Recover from a crash

The Plugin monitors each sensor and restarts it while its tag stays desired. Kill the sensor and wait for a replacement:

```elixir
Process.exit(sensor, :kill)

replacement =
  SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
    case SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.whereis("inbox-1", "support") do
      pid when is_pid(pid) and pid != sensor -> pid
      _other -> nil
    end
  end)

SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.push(replacement, %{message_id: "msg-3"})

SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn -> "msg-3" in Jido.AgentServer.agent(inbox).state.message_ids end)
Jido.AgentServer.agent(inbox).state.message_ids
# => ["msg-1", "msg-2", "msg-3"]
```

A sensor that crashes restarts after `:retry_delay_ms`, the same wait as a failed start. A sensor that keeps crashing therefore starts at most once per delay, and it keeps restarting while its tag stays desired. The delay is fixed. For a longer backoff, handle the fault inside the sensor.

## Change a sensor

Sending `start` again with the same tag and the same module and config keeps the running process. A different module or config replaces it.

```elixir
{:ok, _agent, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(inbox, manage.(:start, "support"))
SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.whereis("inbox-1", "support") == replacement
# => true
```

## Handle a failed start

The Turn commits before the sensor starts, so a start failure does not fail the Turn. The call returns `{:ok, ...}`, the tag stays desired, and the Plugin retries after `:retry_delay_ms`. Read the failure from `last_effect_outcome` in `Jido.AgentServer.status/2`. The call can return before that field updates, so poll it.

```elixir
Application.put_env(:sensor_guide, :mailbox_online, false)

{:ok, _agent, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(inbox, manage.(:start, "billing"))

outcome =
  SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn ->
    case Jido.AgentServer.status(inbox).last_effect_outcome do
      %{status: :failed} = outcome -> outcome
      _other -> nil
    end
  end)

%{status: :failed, stage: :directive} = outcome
Map.keys(Jido.AgentServer.agent(inbox).state.sensors.desired)
# => ["billing", "support"]
```

Bring the dependency back. The retry starts the sensor with no new Directive.

```elixir
Application.put_env(:sensor_guide, :mailbox_online, true)

billing = SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn -> SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.whereis("inbox-1", "billing") end)
is_pid(billing)
# => true
```

## Reject invalid Directives

The tag must be a portable term and not `nil`. The sensor must be a loaded module with `child_spec/1`, and the config must be a portable map. An invalid Directive fails its Turn before commit, so neither state nor sensors change.

```elixir
Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Start.validate(Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.start(nil, SensorGuide.MailboxSensor))
|> elem(1)
|> Exception.message()
# => "Sensor tag must not be nil"
```

```elixir
Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.Start.validate(
  Jido.Plugin.SensorManager.start("support", SensorGuide.MailboxSensor, %{owner: self()})
)
|> elem(1)
|> Exception.message()
# => "Sensor config must contain portable data"
```

Keep live values out of config. Pass names or identifiers that the sensor can resolve when it starts.

## Stop sensors

`stop/1` removes the tag from desired state, and the Plugin terminates the process. All remaining sensors stop with the Agent.

```elixir
{:ok, stopped, _directives} = Jido.AgentServer.call(inbox, manage.(:stop, "support"))
Map.keys(stopped.state.sensors.desired)
# => ["billing"]
```

```elixir
SensorGuide.Wait.until(fn -> SensorGuide.MailboxSensor.whereis("inbox-1", "support") == nil end)

monitor = Process.monitor(billing)
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(SensorGuide.Jido, inbox)

receive do
  {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^billing, reason} -> reason
after
  1_000 -> raise "the sensor did not stop"
end
# => :shutdown
```

Desired sensors live in Agent state. When a durable Agent restores, the Plugin starts them again from that state. In-memory work inside a sensor does not survive, so a sensor must read its position from its source or from `config`.

